Fillets: an analytic blend whose contact reaches the face at the end
exactly at the spine's end vertex, that face going on from the contact's
face tangentially, runs on past the end and is cut by that face
(mitcad#1345).

ChFi3d_KPartCutToEnd cuts an analytic blend by the face at the end B where
its contact on a face N leaves N across an edge E into B before the
spine's end. It was not tried where N and B are tangent along E. A fin's
top, an arc's cylinder N chamfered along its edges, is rounded with 0.2 up
to the fin's end face B, an extrusion that goes on from the top
tangentially across E and falls away from it. The torus between N and the
chamfer's cone ends at the arc's end section, its contact on N reaching E
there, at the vertex V where the arc's edge ends. The stored body's torus
runs on past V on N's surface continued, B cutting it from E on up to where
its other contact meets the cone's edge with B. OCCT ended the torus at V
and closed the end with a corner face that left the cone to meet B
directly, 0.013 off the stored body; on the fin's other side, where the
arc's blend is walked, the walk past V gave the stored body's end.

Where N and B are tangent along E, the end piece P of a fillet lies on the
end edge, ends at V's section with its contact on E, and E runs into V,
the blend is now cut past the end: the point Q where its other contact
meets the edge between its face and B lies beyond V's section, and the
section through Q is found by bisection on the spine run on beyond V (up
to the end edge's length); the piece from Q to P's end, its side on N the
blend's intersection with B, is added before P, which keeps the end edge.
Elsewhere such a tangent end is left to the corner as before, and so are
chamfers (a chamfer's end there is made by its own cuts).

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_fin_end.cpp, the reported fin's top
rounded up to its end face: about the fin's end the result lies on the
stored body within 0.002 mm both ways (0.011 off without the patch).

Upstream: new.

